WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:06.000
This lecture, and the next, are probably the
most explicitly philosophical lectures of

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.100
the course.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:13.299
Then, we move into psychometrics, and biological
psychology.

00:00:13.299 --> 00:00:17.320
Those are going to be the most scientific
lectures of the course.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:25.449
I was thinking, this morning, while I was
preparing this lecture, about why I approach

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:30.460
these topics this way.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:38.360
Part of the answer is, probably, that it is
not clear that the study of personality - at

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:46.910
least, insofar as the concern is to further
the development of personality, which is a

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:51.170
term associated with the desire for mental
health, or "subjective well-being," a term

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:58.480
I really do not like, or "meaning" - can be
strictly scientific pursuits.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:06.159
So, then, if they're not strictly scientific
pursuits, what should you do about understanding

00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:11.140
them?

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:17.070
If you look at studies, after studies, the
problem is you get a very narrow slice of

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.070
the domain.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:23.359
It's often not very comprehensible because,
in order to understand the results of a study,

00:01:23.359 --> 00:01:27.340
you have to have the knowledge - the underlying
knowledge - that is necessary to put the study

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.820
in some sort of framework.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:36.430
That framework is going to be developed by
studying the relevant scientific literature

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.119
and psychological literature.

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:45.500
Behind that, the framework has to be expanded
to include the relevant philosophical assumptions.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.990
I do not really think that you can understand
the details without understanding the assumptions.

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:58.079
I also think you are relegated to memorization
if you do not understand the fundamentals.

00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:01.429
Memorization has very little to do with knowledge.

00:02:01.429 --> 00:02:06.030
You might be able to memorize procedures that
would enable you to act on something, perhaps

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:09.690
to fix an automobile or to play a piece on
the piano.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.400
It is not like those things are not worth
doing.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:21.170
But for these ideas to take root and have
affect and meaning, you have to understand

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:22.410
them at the right level of analysis.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:26.350
One of the things I really like about personality
theory, especially the clinical end of it,

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:31.700
though not exclusively the clinical end of
it, is that the people who were conducting

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:37.220
clinical practice and writing clinical theory
during the 20th century were in fact dealing

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:41.310
with the most profound problems that affect
people.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:46.230
I started my academic career as a political
scientist, while in so far as you're any sort

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.840
of political scientist when you are an undergraduate.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.340
I was not interested in it at all by my third
year because what I found was that, at least

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:59.690
at this time - and I don't know how much it
has changed - the political scientists had

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:04.700
already decided that people were basically
motivated by economic concerns.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:10.240
To me that was no use at all because I wanted
to know why they were motivated by economic

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:11.580
concerns.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:17.820
It is easy to understand people in some sense
if you already decide what they value.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:21.920
But if you can't figure out what they value,
or what they should value, that is a whole

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.340
different issue - and that's psychology.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.470
It is a deep question because it isn't even
obvious whether the question "are there things

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:36.510
you should value" is a reasonable question
or that it can be reasonably answered.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:41.430
The thing I can tell you about that is most
closely allied with my own experience.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:48.080
I do not mean personal experience, but say,
experience as a clinician, is that aimless

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.070
people are in real trouble.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:53.430
Now I do not necessarily know why that is.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:59.880
And I do not necessarily know what that means
for what your aim should be, but I have certainly

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:00.880
seen,

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.220
for example, if you had to make a choice which
all of you will in the next five years or

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:12.500
so between pursuing something diligently and
establishing a fixed identity because of that,

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:17.190
or remaining bereft of choice and drifting.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:21.189
I can tell you that if you drift, by the time
you are 30, you are going to be one miserable

00:04:21.189 --> 00:04:22.280
person.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:26.830
Now I am not sure why that is exactly, and
I am not exactly sure that that necessarily

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:31.090
means that picking something and sticking
to it, which is a form of apprenticeship,

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.270
is better than drifting.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:36.099
It depends what you mean by better.

00:04:36.099 --> 00:04:41.259
But I can tell you that not catalyzing an
identity seems to be a mistake, and it is

00:04:41.259 --> 00:04:42.960
a fatal mistake by the time you are 40.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.770
It is very difficult to recover from it at
that point.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.629
because you are not young anymore, at that
point.

00:04:49.629 --> 00:04:53.840
If you try to catalyze an identity at that
time, which sometimes can happen, you are

00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:00.020
competing with all these young shiny people,
who are fuller of potential from the perspective

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.600
of an employer, for example, then you are.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:06.509
It gets pretty dismal.

00:05:06.509 --> 00:05:13.430
Anyways, today we're going to go deeper into
philosophical presuppositions than we have

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:17.030
in the past.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:21.860
I want to familiarize you with what I think
are the great philosophical and psychological

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:25.770
movements of the 20th century because they
shape you and they shape the world you live

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:31.259
in, in ways that are incalculable.

00:05:31.259 --> 00:05:34.270
If you do not understand them, you do not
really know where you are.

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:38.590
You do not know where you are in history and
you do not know what ideas you are possessed

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:39.590
by.

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:45.220
I think I told you when we were studying Jung,
that Jung said that - people do not have ideas,

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:49.770
that ideas have people - which I believe to
be true.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:55.860
One of Jung's lasting contributions in some
sense, was that you should know what ideas

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:01.979
possess you because otherwise you will not
know what the hell they are doing with you.

00:06:01.979 --> 00:06:06.210
When you think about all the irrational and
apparently counterproductive things that people

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:13.169
do as individuals and also in a mass, you
have to ask yourself if you want to be caught

00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:14.539
up in that sort of thing.

00:06:14.539 --> 00:06:20.900
If you could be free from it, if you are caught
up in it, just exactly where is it that you

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:21.900
are headed.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.889
Which was also something that Jung thought
you should figure out in case where you are

00:06:25.889 --> 00:06:32.699
headed was not necessarily where you would
go if you were making a fully informed conscious

00:06:32.699 --> 00:06:33.699
choice.

00:06:33.699 --> 00:06:38.699
I think that material that we're dealing within
the next two lectures, is the most relevant

00:06:38.699 --> 00:06:47.379
of all the material were going to cover with
regards to the possession of people by ideas.

00:06:47.379 --> 00:06:54.159
The existentialists, who are tightly aligned
philosophically with the phenomenologists,

00:06:54.159 --> 00:07:02.979
basically emerged as a psychological movement
after World War II.There are reasons for this.

00:07:02.979 --> 00:07:08.000
One of the reasons was that it was quite obvious,
not only that World War II was an ideological

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:15.259
battle fundamentally between Fascism and Western
democracy roughly speaking,

00:07:15.259 --> 00:07:20.089
and it was immediately supplanted by another
ideological battle, which was the one between

00:07:20.089 --> 00:07:23.229
communism and liberalism, roughly speaking.

00:07:23.229 --> 00:07:30.800
The issue of ideological possession and the
relationship between the individual, who is

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:36.580
ideologically possessed, and their responsibility
and the actions of the state, became paramount

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:39.990
concerns in the 1950s, as they should have.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:45.999
One of the lasting questions that remained
after World War II that still has been insufficiently

00:07:45.999 --> 00:07:47.960
answered is,

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:56.770
when the mass goes insane, what is the culpability
of the individuals who compose the mass?

00:07:56.770 --> 00:08:01.620
Now you can circumvent that question with
regards to what happened in Nazi Germany by

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:08.300
assuming that it was top-down coercion that
turned the mass of ordinary German citizens

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:11.789
into majority Nazis.

00:08:11.789 --> 00:08:15.369
I do not think that there is any evidence
that those sorts of ideas are true.

00:08:15.369 --> 00:08:21.830
There is research bearing on people's willingness
to conform to authority figures.

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:27.630
You know the famous experiments on the prison
experiment.

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:33.450
For example, at Stanford, where undergraduates
were divided arbitrarily into guards and prisoners,

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:38.940
and then they ran a simulation of the prison,
and of course the guards turned into sadistic

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:40.740
psychopaths,

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:41.849
some of them did anyways.

00:08:41.849 --> 00:08:45.660
The prisoners turned into cringing victims
in no time flat.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:52.180
There is obviously an element there that demonstrates
that people are very responsive to situational

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:56.880
cues and that they can go out of hand very
rapidly.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:05.589
But that does not necessarily mean that you
can use your tendency to be accommodating

00:09:05.589 --> 00:09:10.890
to authority or the human tendency to be accommodating
to authority, as an explanation for the rise

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:18.800
of mass movements like Nazism or communism,
because the explanation does not really help.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:22.040
Okay, some people in the mass were mere followers.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:23.660
What about the leaders?

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:28.269
Well, maybe they were all followers' right
up to Hitler, so it is Hitler's fault.

00:09:28.269 --> 00:09:29.480
It is all Hitler's fault?

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:33.459
You are elevating the guy of the status of
a God at that point.

00:09:33.459 --> 00:09:40.460
Now an evil God, but still, if he has got
all the motive power, you cannot separate

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:41.990
him from the idea of Lucifer.

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:45.250
He has become an archetypical figure of evil
at that point.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:47.340
It's the same with Stalin and Mao.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.360
We know that they were very, very bad men.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:52.480
There is no doubt about it.

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:58.330
But to localize all the evil in them and to
consider everyone else victimized followers,

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:06.149
is a convenient idea, but it is not helpful.

00:10:06.149 --> 00:10:10.190
That just makes the followers pathetic for
a different reason.

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:16.440
They're not actively self-engaged in cruelty
for their own purposes apart from conformity,

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:20.130
but they are just as pathetic and evil as
they would be if they were doing it on their

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:21.930
own volition.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:24.579
I do not see the difference between a bully
and a bully's henchmen.

00:10:24.579 --> 00:10:28.560
In fact, I think the bully probably has more
courage than the henchmen.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:35.480
It is courage of a fairly peculiar sort.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:38.610
This is what the existentialists were concerned
about.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:51.580
The locus of their concern was 
basically Nietzsche.

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:57.420
You all know that the reason I concentrate
on Nietzsche and also on Dostoyevsky's because

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:00.519
I think those two people summed up the 19th
century.

00:11:00.519 --> 00:11:03.410
I really think that.

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:12.300
The problems that they laid out and predicted
would unfold in the 20th century, were the

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:14.639
problems that unfolded in the 20th century.

00:11:14.639 --> 00:11:20.010
They got their predictions right and I think
they got their causality right too.

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:25.630
Given the inability of social scientists,
including psychologists, to predict large

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:31.490
term mass events, the fact that these two
people managed at 30 to 40 years before the

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:36.259
events unfolded and even longer that not seems
to me that it is pretty much worthwhile to

00:11:36.259 --> 00:11:39.220
consider them psychologists.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:40.709
Certainly Nietzsche thought that of himself.

00:11:40.709 --> 00:11:44.279
And so did Dostoyevsky for that matter, and
they had immense influence on people like

00:11:44.279 --> 00:11:49.269
Freud and Jung and Rogers, all the people
that we have been studying.

00:11:49.269 --> 00:11:55.490
Their thinking is lying underneath every issue
we have discussed.

00:11:55.490 --> 00:12:01.490
This is one of Nietzsche's great statements.

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:05.379
Of what is great one must either be silent
or speak with greatness.

00:12:05.379 --> 00:12:08.319
With greatness, that means cynically and with
innocence.

00:12:08.319 --> 00:12:13.779
What I relate is the history of the next two
centuries.

00:12:13.779 --> 00:12:19.509
I describe what is coming, what can no longer
come differently, the advent of nihilism.

00:12:19.509 --> 00:12:23.149
Our whole European culture is moving from
some time now, with a tortured tension that

00:12:23.149 --> 00:12:29.879
is growing from decade to decade, as toward
a catastrophe restlessly, violently, headlong,

00:12:29.879 --> 00:12:33.350
like a river that wants to reach the end,
that no longer reflects.

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:35.790
It's afraid to reflect.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:41.139
He that speaks here has, conversely, done
nothing so far but to reflect as a philosopher

00:12:41.139 --> 00:12:48.240
and solitary by instinct, who was found his
advantage in standing aside, outside.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:53.550
Why has the advent of nihilism become necessary?

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:58.570
Because the values we have had hitherto thus
draw their final consequence.

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:04.529
Because nihilism represents the ultimate logical
extension of our great values and ideals.

00:13:04.529 --> 00:13:08.920
Because we must experience nihilism before
we can find out what value these values really

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:10.959
had.

00:13:10.959 --> 00:13:17.920
So one of Nietzsche's claims, for example,
was that as Christianity in Europe transformed

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:27.160
itself into science, he felt that one of the
advantage to the Catholic domination of Europe

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:34.060
for so many centuries, was that the mind of
the Catholic adherents who took the discipline

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:41.269
seriously, or the dogma seriously, learned
to interpret all events under the under the

00:13:41.269 --> 00:13:43.800
schema of a single theory.

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:46.190
He thought about that as a form of discipline.

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:52.779
Imagine that if I want to teach you how to
theorize, I might teach you a theory and have

00:13:52.779 --> 00:13:55.939
you adopt it.

00:13:55.939 --> 00:13:59.790
Nietzsche's point would be that while you
know a theory, but it also means something

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:00.790
else.

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:06.740
It also means that now you know how to theorize
and the important consequence of learning

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:08.139
a theory may not be the theory.

00:14:08.139 --> 00:14:11.560
It may be that you learn to theorize.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:15.269
Nietzsche also pointed out that once you learn
to theorize, you can separate yourself from

00:14:15.269 --> 00:14:18.360
the theory that gave rise to that knowledge.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:22.740
And so you can start to theorize even about
the theory that you mastered, and he thought

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:27.329
that is what happened to Europe as a consequence
of its domination by Christianity, especially

00:14:27.329 --> 00:14:32.269
because of Christianity's essential insistence
on the utility of the truth.

00:14:32.269 --> 00:14:39.029
He thought that was transformed after Catholicism,
into scientific investigation, but that the

00:14:39.029 --> 00:14:41.939
Spirit of theorizing in truth, remained intact.

00:14:41.939 --> 00:14:46.699
The consequence of that was that the European
mind was disciplined by a dogma.

00:14:46.699 --> 00:14:51.579
That it freed itself from the dogma, that
it turned its power on the dogma, and noted

00:14:51.579 --> 00:14:57.430
that the dogma itself, seemed to be grounded
in nothing that you could get a grip on.

00:14:57.430 --> 00:15:00.490
The way you grip things with an empirical
mind.

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:03.300
And so it fell apart.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:09.610
That's not saying much more than science posed
a fatal challenge to religion, but it's saying

00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:12.740
it in a much more profound and interesting
way.

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:17.470
It also explains why he makes this claim,
that nihilism is the logical conclusion of

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:18.810
the great values and ideals.

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:24.480
He did not think about nihilism as a counter
proposition, say to dogmatic Christianity.

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:30.649
He thought about it as the logical outcome
of that.

00:15:30.649 --> 00:15:31.810
Is that relevant?

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:33.310
Why is that relevant?

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:37.790
Well think it's relevant for a lot of reasons.

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:44.149
The first observation might be that a tremendous
amount of mental illness, this is an existential

00:15:44.149 --> 00:15:47.199
claim, is grounded in nihilism.

00:15:47.199 --> 00:15:51.949
When someone who is depressed, comes to see
you, what they often say is, I cannot see

00:15:51.949 --> 00:15:54.600
any point in life.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:56.759
That isn't what they mean.

00:15:56.759 --> 00:16:03.019
What they mean is, they see the meaning of
life as suffering, which is a meaning, right?

00:16:03.019 --> 00:16:04.999
And that is not bearable.

00:16:04.999 --> 00:16:09.640
And then the question is, why bother with
it?

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:11.759
And that is the fundamental question of suicide.

00:16:11.759 --> 00:16:13.639
It is a philosophical question.

00:16:13.639 --> 00:16:18.002
I think it was Camus who said, the only real
philosophical question was whether or not

00:16:18.002 --> 00:16:19.690
to commit suicide.

00:16:19.690 --> 00:16:24.560
Now you know, that is a little dark, coming
like well maybe Camus could use some SSRI's,

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:26.970
but you get the point.

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:36.509
And it is inappropriate, in my estimation,
to even discuss depression with someone who

00:16:36.509 --> 00:16:42.470
is depressed, especially if they are intelligent
and open, and therefore more tilted towards

00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:46.650
philosophical wanderings without actually
addressing the issue.

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:48.470
Why live in the face of suffering?

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:54.790
Okay, so that is one problem.To the degree
that you will find it difficult in your life

00:16:54.790 --> 00:17:01.459
to build anything solid under your feet that
you can stand on and believe, have faith in,

00:17:01.459 --> 00:17:03.959
let's say, you are going to be adrift.

00:17:03.959 --> 00:17:08.079
The reason for that is a lot of the things
are going to have to do will be difficult

00:17:08.079 --> 00:17:11.959
and they will involve suffering, which is
also an existential claim.

00:17:11.959 --> 00:17:15.949
So, the existentialist for example, they do
not make the same claim Freud does.

00:17:15.949 --> 00:17:23.270
Freud claims that, in some sense that the
normal person is mentally healthy apart from

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:28.710
the mild distress of normal life, and in order
to be psychopathological, you have to have

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:31.320
been hurt,

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:35.660
maybe multiple times or, there are other things
that could contribute to that the existentialist

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:39.540
would say no, no, let us just wait a minute
here.

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:46.159
Maybe the fundamental condition of human beings
is nihilism and suffering, and that something

00:17:46.159 --> 00:17:52.120
has to be produced to counter that in order
for life to be tolerable.

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:55.940
Well, I think that is a perfectly reasonable
proposition.

00:17:55.940 --> 00:18:01.890
It's a strange proposition because I have
seen in my lifetime, people who are tormented

00:18:01.890 --> 00:18:04.950
by existential ideas who cannot get them out
of their mind.

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:09.730
You know, ideas that relate to the meaning
of life, other people and concern about death,

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:13.510
for example, and the extinguishing of everything
that seems to have any value.

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:15.659
It is a primary concern with them.

00:18:15.659 --> 00:18:18.900
I have seen other people for whom those questions
never seem to arise.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:26.010
Now, I think those people are conservative
people, not very open and probably rather

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:27.210
low with neuroticism.

00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:29.260
They are not philosophically curious.

00:18:29.260 --> 00:18:32.410
They do not go up chains of abstractions.

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:37.290
Even if they do, they do not necessarily get
disturbed in the most profound areas of their

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:39.529
being by the questioning.

00:18:39.529 --> 00:18:51.080
That still leaves plenty of people in the
other category.

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:52.870
Nihilism and atheism are closely related.

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:56.059
They are not identical by any stretch of the
imagination.

00:18:56.059 --> 00:19:02.330
Although I think it is difficult for atheism
to describe why it is not essentially nihilistic.

00:19:02.330 --> 00:19:05.350
That is Dostoyevsky's big criticism.

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:12.220
Dostoyevsky's claim was that without any fundamental
value assumed, then there is no reason why

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:15.180
you cannot do anything you want.

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:16.419
And that is his famous line.

00:19:16.419 --> 00:19:18.740
"If there is no God, then everything is permitted".

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:25.870
All of Dostoyevsky's novel writing is an exploration
of that idea.

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:29.380
Sometimes it is an exploration of what that
idea might mean if it was acted out in the

00:19:29.380 --> 00:19:32.990
life of the given individual, Crime and Punishment.

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:36.850
Another would be, in his book, The Devils
or The Possessed.

00:19:36.850 --> 00:19:43.350
It is an examination of what that idea means
if it is gripped by an individual who has

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:44.960
social and political ambitions.

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:51.110
That is when Dostoyevsky basically prophesied,
so to speak, that one of the consequences

00:19:51.110 --> 00:19:56.240
of the death of God would be the rise, basically,
of Communist of totalitarianism because essentially,

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:59.100
that is what he predicted in The Devils.

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:01.600
It is pretty dead on accurate prediction.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:04.679
It was really quite stunning to me when I
came across it.

00:20:04.679 --> 00:20:08.270
Nietzsche made exactly the same prediction,
by the way.

00:20:08.270 --> 00:20:13.549
For those two men, the death of an ultimate
meaning system, especially one that you see

00:20:13.549 --> 00:20:17.549
when you think about something like European
Christianity at its misleading, in some sense,

00:20:17.549 --> 00:20:24.250
because the system of beliefs that that constituted
European Christianity and other great belief

00:20:24.250 --> 00:20:27.120
systems wasn't 2000 years old.

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:30.160
It was 25,000 years old.

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:36.730
You know you can think about it as beginning
at year zero but it is a mistake from a historical

00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:37.730
perspective.

00:20:37.730 --> 00:20:44.529
The ideas that profound religious traditions
are predicated on are generally grounded in

00:20:44.529 --> 00:20:49.029
ideas that are much older than the traditions
themselves.

00:20:49.029 --> 00:20:54.240
In some sense when at the end of the 19th
century when things fell apart for us and

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:59.860
we can no longer rely on our history predicated
morality to guide us.

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:04.950
It wasn't merely that we lost an overlay,
a psychological overlay that it be laid on

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:06.700
humanity for 2000 years.

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:09.270
It was way deeper than that.

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:12.429
We do not even know how old those how will
those ideas are.

00:21:12.429 --> 00:21:14.620
We know we have some idea about how old they
are there.

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:17.530
They are at least as old as written culture.

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:22.370
But we also know that the people who have
been brought into the main streams of history,

00:21:22.370 --> 00:21:28.169
you know, as the world has united people who
were not literate had mythologies that drew

00:21:28.169 --> 00:21:29.510
from the same themes.

00:21:29.510 --> 00:21:34.700
Some of those people, as far as we can tell,
had lived a lifestyle that was essentially

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:37.399
unchanged for 25,000 years.

00:21:37.399 --> 00:21:41.049
Australian aborigines are like that.

00:21:41.049 --> 00:21:45.100
There is plenty of evidence that these ideas
are extraordinarily old.

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:48.779
What that means is, when we separate from
them in some sense, not only do we separate

00:21:48.779 --> 00:21:55.690
from our philosophical presuppositions, but
we separate from the historical consequences

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:56.690
of our biology.

00:21:56.690 --> 00:21:59.039
It is a serious problem.

00:21:59.039 --> 00:22:04.529
I think that is partly why it is very difficult
to distinguish between someone who is nihilistic

00:22:04.529 --> 00:22:06.940
and someone who is mentally ill.

00:22:06.940 --> 00:22:10.480
It is not a radical claim.

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:15.130
People, especially those on the depressed
side of the distribution, will tell you that

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:16.130
they are nihilistic.

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:18.870
They may not use that terminology, although
they often do.

00:22:18.870 --> 00:22:25.289
I just cannot see any point, what why does
that matter or why does it matter.

00:22:25.289 --> 00:22:27.140
It seems to be a fact that it matters.

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:32.490
It is an interesting fact, that is a phenomenological
fact in some sense, because one of the things

00:22:32.490 --> 00:22:39.860
that Heidegger pointed out, he was a founder
of the phenomenological school, was that your

00:22:39.860 --> 00:22:44.600
primary orientation to the world, he thinks
in a strange way, that your primary orientation

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:47.730
to the world was one of care.

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:50.720
You could say, what characterizes your experience?

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:52.860
What sort of creature are you?

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:57.771
Heidegger's answer would be, you are a creature
who cares about things, in so far as your

00:22:57.771 --> 00:23:01.950
engaged in the world, your primary orientation
is one of care.

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:03.900
You can think about that as a value.

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:06.720
It is a consequence of your value orientation.

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:09.260
God only knows where that comes from.

00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:12.690
Part of it is biological, part of it is developmental,
part of it is historical.

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:15.059
It is very, very complex.

00:23:15.059 --> 00:23:19.409
But if you stop caring about everything, you
are in trouble.

00:23:19.409 --> 00:23:24.720
That is one of the things that seems to indicate
that caring is actually a fundamental reality.

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:27.909
You stop caring about things, you do not stop
suffering.

00:23:27.909 --> 00:23:32.899
It seems that unless the caring counterbalances
the suffering, you cannot maintain an even

00:23:32.899 --> 00:23:33.899
keel.

00:23:33.899 --> 00:23:35.140
That is partly because it does not seem just.

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:40.320
When terrible things happen to people, they
always say two things.

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:44.200
How it is that being could be constituted
in this manner?

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:48.090
What the hell's going on at the fundamental
levels of reality, that such suffering has

00:23:48.090 --> 00:23:49.090
to be the case?

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:54.540
You will certainly ask that if you have a
child was diagnosed with cancer, for example.

00:23:54.540 --> 00:24:00.640
Or you might think, why is this cruelty as
it appears necessarily aimed at me right now

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:04.890
in this place, when hypothetically it could
have not happened at all, or perhaps been

00:24:04.890 --> 00:24:08.290
visited on someone more deserving.

00:24:08.290 --> 00:24:12.740
Which is the good remain the good are punished
and the evil remain unpunished something like

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:14.590
that.

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:22.840
For human beings that produces a 
cry for justice.

00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:24.960
How can the world be constituted that way?

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:26.409
That seems to be built into us.

00:24:26.409 --> 00:24:28.269
Those aren't questions we can just avoid.

00:24:28.269 --> 00:24:30.529
They're questions that will arise in your
psyche.

00:24:30.529 --> 00:24:37.060
They will arise as fundamental questions when
sufficiently terrible things happen to you.

00:24:37.060 --> 00:24:40.970
So the existentialists would say, those are
conditions of existence, you are just stuck

00:24:40.970 --> 00:24:41.970
with that.

00:24:41.970 --> 00:24:43.380
It's part of human nature.

00:24:43.380 --> 00:24:48.890
It's part of human being to be perplexed by
those questions.

00:24:48.890 --> 00:24:53.520
Then the question is, at least in part, is
there any way of answering them?

00:24:53.520 --> 00:25:00.640
Nietzsche said, we required some time, new
values.

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:02.720
Nihilism stands at the door.

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:06.830
Whence comes this uncanniest of all guests?

00:25:06.830 --> 00:25:09.409
Point of departure.

00:25:09.409 --> 00:25:13.380
It is an error to consider social distress
or physiological degeneration or corruption

00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:16.269
of all things as the cause of nihilism.

00:25:16.269 --> 00:25:22.820
Now that is a typical Nietzschian phrase because
there are three profound ideas in that sentence.

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:26.010
Each one is in a different phrase.

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:30.330
Nietzsche said at one point, I can write in
a sentence what other people write in a book.

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:34.890
Then he said, what other people cannot even
write in a book.

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:36.320
This sentence is a good example of that.

00:25:36.320 --> 00:25:38.880
So what is he say?

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:43.760
if you see that people are suffering and in
trouble, one thing you can say that is that

00:25:43.760 --> 00:25:48.190
the reason for that is that the economic system
is unjust and they are layered along the bottom

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:51.210
and that is the fundamental cause of their
suffering.

00:25:51.210 --> 00:25:56.860
But Nietzsche does not allow that to be a
causal interpretation because he says there

00:25:56.860 --> 00:26:00.519
are multiple ways of interpreting your position.

00:26:00.519 --> 00:26:08.210
Near absence of material luxury does not necessarily
destine you to one perspective or another.

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:10.019
physiological degeneration.

00:26:10.019 --> 00:26:14.890
People are unhappy or suffering because they
are ill in some manner.

00:26:14.890 --> 00:26:19.380
You could make that a matter of definition
by saying, that if you're suffering or unhappy,

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:20.620
you are ill.

00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:22.390
But that is not a causal argument.

00:26:22.390 --> 00:26:24.990
It is just a different way of categorizing
the data.

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:31.679
Nietzche would reject that because he would
also note that there is some correlation between

00:26:31.679 --> 00:26:35.000
physiological health and meaning in life.

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:38.899
But the correlation does not imply causality.

00:26:38.899 --> 00:26:43.500
Even if it did, the relationship is by no
means perfect to the degree that you would

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:48.460
want a relationship to be before you accepted
it as relevant.

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:53.000
Or corruption of all things, that would be
the idea that being itself is evil, like an

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:57.470
evil trick, which is what Tolstoy said, by
the way, when he wrote his confessions.

00:26:57.470 --> 00:27:01.799
Tolstoy, at the height of his intellectual
power, he was the most famous novelist in

00:27:01.799 --> 00:27:05.820
the world and unbelievably well regarded well
throughout the world, but particularly in

00:27:05.820 --> 00:27:06.820
Russia.

00:27:06.820 --> 00:27:11.080
He was a very socially benevolent man and
well regarded for his wisdom.

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:15.210
For years he was afraid to go outside with
a rope or a gun because he thought he would

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:17.130
either hang or shoot himself.

00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:25.820
The reason for that was that he had been struck
by the idea that life is so unbearable, that

00:27:25.820 --> 00:27:26.820
it should be eradicated.

00:27:26.820 --> 00:27:34.230
He could not think his way out of that.

00:27:34.230 --> 00:27:39.409
it was a form of thought that was actually
very characteristic of intellectuals in Russia

00:27:39.409 --> 00:27:44.820
during his time and in his place.

00:27:44.820 --> 00:27:50.580
Dostoyevsky wrote about exactly the same sorts
of things.

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:55.950
Even Tolstoy noticed that merely observing
that the world was a corrupt and evil place

00:27:55.950 --> 00:27:59.860
was not necessarily enough to tilt people
towards nihilism because there seem to be

00:27:59.860 --> 00:28:05.340
people who weren't nihilistic despite the
fact that that seem self-evident to him.

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:10.940
Tolstoy actually turned to the Russian people,
you know what he was very entranced by the

00:28:10.940 --> 00:28:17.419
idea of the folk and folk wisdom, and he turned
to the Russian people as a source of new inspiration

00:28:17.419 --> 00:28:19.269
like the peasantry.

00:28:19.269 --> 00:28:23.340
Tolstoy actually fought for the freedom of
the peasantry and he felt that their simple

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:30.080
faith, so to speak, was something truly admirable
rather than something pathetic and weak from

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:31.409
an intellectual perspective.

00:28:31.409 --> 00:28:36.389
He strove to emulate that criticism-less faith.

00:28:36.389 --> 00:28:40.550
But of course he could not do it because once
you taking a bite out of apple, there is no

00:28:40.550 --> 00:28:42.789
going back, so to speak.

00:28:42.789 --> 00:28:47.409
Nietzsche says, distress, whether psychic,
physical or intellectual, need not at all

00:28:47.409 --> 00:28:48.860
produce nihilism.

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:52.340
That is, the radical rejection of value, meaning
and desirability.

00:28:52.340 --> 00:28:55.700
Such distress always permits a variety of
interpretations.

00:28:55.700 --> 00:29:00.049
Rather, it is one particular interpretation,
the Christian moral one, that nihilism is

00:29:00.049 --> 00:29:01.049
rooted.

00:29:01.049 --> 00:29:06.090
The end of Christianity, at the hands of its
own morality, which cannot be replaced.

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:07.809
Which turns against the Christian God.

00:29:07.809 --> 00:29:12.620
The sense of truthfulness, highly developed
by Christianity is nauseated by the falseness

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:17.630
and mendaciousness of all Christian interpretations
of the world and of history.

00:29:17.630 --> 00:29:23.059
It is a rebound from God is the truth to the
equally fanatical faith.

00:29:23.059 --> 00:29:24.389
All is false.

00:29:24.389 --> 00:29:26.950
An act of Buddhism.

00:29:26.950 --> 00:29:30.649
Scepticism regarding morality is what is decisive.

00:29:30.649 --> 00:29:34.820
The end of the moral interpretation of the
world, which no longer has any sanction after

00:29:34.820 --> 00:29:38.580
it's tried to escape into some beyond, leads
to nihilism.

00:29:38.580 --> 00:29:39.660
All lacks meaning.

00:29:39.660 --> 00:29:44.360
That is rooted in Nietzsche's criticism of
Christianity because he believed that Christianity

00:29:44.360 --> 00:29:49.350
was exceptionally morally flawed because all
it offered its followers was the possibility

00:29:49.350 --> 00:29:54.550
of salvation and redemption from their suffering
after they were dead.

00:29:54.550 --> 00:29:57.840
It was projected into some other world.

00:29:57.840 --> 00:30:02.130
As far as Nietzsche was concerned, alleviated
people of their local responsibility to try

00:30:02.130 --> 00:30:04.090
to improve things here and now

00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:10.269
and Jung's comments about that were essentially,
that it was the proto-scientists recognition

00:30:10.269 --> 00:30:16.309
of the fact that the spiritual salvation that
Christianity promised was no longer sufficient,

00:30:16.309 --> 00:30:18.840
that motivated the development of science.

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:24.450
So, for the early Christians this is part
of the tension between Christianity and science.

00:30:24.450 --> 00:30:31.160
For the early Christians, the idea was that
the earth in some sense was ineradicably corrupt.

00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:36.730
That all you can hope for in your earthly
life was suffering and that you should accept

00:30:36.730 --> 00:30:42.710
your suffering and hope for salvation in the
future after you're dead.

00:30:42.710 --> 00:30:47.460
Obviously that philosophy appeared insufficient
for people.

00:30:47.460 --> 00:30:52.700
In Jung's hypothesis about the development
of science was that a counter fantasy developed

00:30:52.700 --> 00:30:56.269
in the unconscious of the Europeans which
was that

00:30:56.269 --> 00:31:02.090
the material realm, which had been defined
as evil, and therefore not worthy of any study

00:31:02.090 --> 00:31:07.750
or any pursuit whatsoever actually held the
seeds of the redemption that was lacking.

00:31:07.750 --> 00:31:12.950
That was Jung's commentary on the idea of
the philosopher's stone because the alchemists,

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:18.350
who were proto-scientists, were trying to
find a material substance that would be the

00:31:18.350 --> 00:31:27.710
philosopher's stone that would offer its holders
wealth, health and eternal life.

00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:29.889
Why are we pursuing science?

00:31:29.889 --> 00:31:36.409
Well, hopefully, because we think it will
do us some good here and now, in our bodies.

00:31:36.409 --> 00:31:43.679
Jung regarded science itself as stemming from
that compensatory dream, brilliant idea.

00:31:43.679 --> 00:31:49.500
It is actually the only idea I have ever read
that seems to do a reasonable psychological

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:53.050
account for the emergence of science as a
discipline.

00:31:53.050 --> 00:31:55.290
It is a very strange practice.

00:31:55.290 --> 00:32:01.149
You have to narrow your interests tremendously
to be a scientist.

00:32:01.149 --> 00:32:06.490
You have to focus on one set of phenomena
that might appear as useless to contemplate

00:32:06.490 --> 00:32:09.080
as how many angels can dance on the head of
a pin.

00:32:09.080 --> 00:32:14.809
You have to devote decades to the study of
that thing to make incremental progress.

00:32:14.809 --> 00:32:17.720
Why in the world would people ever be motivated
to do that?

00:32:17.720 --> 00:32:24.240
Jung's interpretation was, there was a deep
counter movement towards the over spiritualisation

00:32:24.240 --> 00:32:31.100
of the psyche and that was the revaluation
of matter and its possibilities.

00:32:31.100 --> 00:32:35.490
While Nietzsche believed that Christianity
as it stood, at the end of the 1800s, was

00:32:35.490 --> 00:32:40.900
an untenable philosophy because he thought
it had abandoned its moral obligations by

00:32:40.900 --> 00:32:46.370
escaping into some beyond, and therefore damned
life as it was actually lived by human beings.

00:32:46.370 --> 00:32:51.700
He felt that the demise was a good thing.

00:32:51.700 --> 00:32:53.480
He points out one other thing.

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:57.870
This is the difference between having a theory
and then learning to theorize.

00:32:57.870 --> 00:33:01.769
He says look, if you have been raised in the
tradition, whatever that tradition happens

00:33:01.769 --> 00:33:06.370
to be, you have a belief system, whatever
that belief system happens to be, and it falls

00:33:06.370 --> 00:33:08.940
apart on you, at any one point

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:10.830
you suffer for two reasons.

00:33:10.830 --> 00:33:15.789
The first is, your belief system fell apart
and that is not a good thing.

00:33:15.789 --> 00:33:20.200
It leaves everything unfixed and open, and
you drown in possibility, in a sense.

00:33:20.200 --> 00:33:22.340
That is a Kierkegaard phrase.

00:33:22.340 --> 00:33:24.890
But the second consequence is even worse.

00:33:24.890 --> 00:33:28.510
Once you've learned that one belief system
that is solid could be demolished and fall

00:33:28.510 --> 00:33:33.799
apart, then it's very difficult ever again
to have any faith in any belief systems whatsoever.

00:33:33.799 --> 00:33:39.299
Not only do you become a doubter of your own
creed, you become a Meta doubter, which is

00:33:39.299 --> 00:33:41.190
the doubter of all belief systems.

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:45.740
The step from that to nihilism, maybe those
are exactly the same thing.

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:50.730
You could think about that in some sense as
the disease of the critical rational mind.

00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:53.890
It can saw off any branch that it is sitting
on.

00:33:53.890 --> 00:33:56.299
And you know the utility of that is?

00:33:56.299 --> 00:33:57.880
Leave no stone unturned, right?

00:33:57.880 --> 00:33:59.350
You are supposed to question things.

00:33:59.350 --> 00:34:02.000
The utility of that is, you learn new things.

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:08.409
But the price you pay for it, is that you
are not necessarily ever certain about anything.

00:34:08.409 --> 00:34:13.190
You could say, maybe you shouldn't be certain
about anything but you can forget that.

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:17.889
You are going to have to act as if you are
certain many times in your life.

00:34:17.889 --> 00:34:22.210
When you choose a permanent mate, for example,
if you do that which you probably will, because

00:34:22.210 --> 00:34:26.520
you're university educated and university
educated people still do that.

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:28.310
Although no one else does.

00:34:28.310 --> 00:34:33.220
So, you are going to pick a career and you
are going to make decisions, one after the

00:34:33.220 --> 00:34:36.770
other about which, if you are not certain,
you cannot make.

00:34:36.770 --> 00:34:38.679
In which case you have no life.

00:34:38.679 --> 00:34:44.149
You are just a whirlwind of chaos, so you
are stuck with the necessity of following

00:34:44.149 --> 00:34:50.359
a course of action, which is acted out certainty
that your intellect cannot regard as appropriate.

00:34:50.359 --> 00:34:53.280
And that is hard on people.

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:56.600
Why should I choose this instead of this?

00:34:56.600 --> 00:35:00.850
Why should I act this way instead of that
way?

00:35:00.850 --> 00:35:05.340
I do not know is not a very useful answer
when you are a creature that is cognitively

00:35:05.340 --> 00:35:13.420
able, as we are.

00:35:13.420 --> 00:35:15.670
This is something absolutely brilliant.

00:35:15.670 --> 00:35:22.069
It is very difficult for me to believe that
it was written so long ago.

00:35:22.069 --> 00:35:30.760
So this is Dostoyevsky's criticism of communism
forty years before communism was a political

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:32.020
force.

00:35:32.020 --> 00:35:35.400
Dostoyevsky's thinking really hard about this
nihilism problem.

00:35:35.400 --> 00:35:37.930
By the way, Nietzsche read Dostoyevsky quite
extensively.

00:35:37.930 --> 00:35:39.071
He is thinking about it.

00:35:39.071 --> 00:35:42.550
He thinks well, there seems to be two alternatives.

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:46.690
One is this superhuman nihilism, which is
sort of a variant of what Nietzsche proposed,

00:35:46.690 --> 00:35:50.619
because Nietzsche proposed that

00:35:50.619 --> 00:35:54.859
it would become the responsibility of every
human being after the death of their religious

00:35:54.859 --> 00:35:58.180
tradition, to establish their own values.

00:35:58.180 --> 00:36:02.430
He did not think people could do it.

00:36:02.430 --> 00:36:06.480
He thought there'd have to be a new kind of
person who could manage it, because you know,

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:13.230
he is basically asking you to generate a coherent
and pragmatically applicable philosophical

00:36:13.230 --> 00:36:17.470
structure, out of nothing, during your lifetime.

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:18.720
Good luck with that.

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:24.190
You know, plus, he assumed that people create
values or that they could create values.

00:36:24.190 --> 00:36:25.630
That is true to some degree.

00:36:25.630 --> 00:36:29.300
We'll talk about this more when we get into
the phenomenological end of things.

00:36:29.300 --> 00:36:31.210
But it is not self-evident, right?

00:36:31.210 --> 00:36:35.820
Because one of the things you may notice is
that you cannot force yourself to love someone,

00:36:35.820 --> 00:36:36.820
right?

00:36:36.820 --> 00:36:40.890
But you cannot just decide to value someone
and then, poof, that happens.

00:36:40.890 --> 00:36:45.960
In fact, you may want with all your heart,
or at least with all your mind, to value someone

00:36:45.960 --> 00:36:47.240
because they deserve it.

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:49.030
They had never mistreated you.

00:36:49.030 --> 00:36:52.700
Maybe you've said you would be loyal to them,
and poof, someone comes along and you're tremendously

00:36:52.700 --> 00:36:59.300
attracted to them and off you go, like someone
who is possessed.

00:36:59.300 --> 00:37:02.829
Well, did you create that value?

00:37:02.829 --> 00:37:08.410
And then, closer to your own experience, can
you actually make yourself interested in something

00:37:08.410 --> 00:37:10.760
you are bored about?

00:37:10.760 --> 00:37:11.980
Good luck trying that, you know.

00:37:11.980 --> 00:37:15.940
You'd rather clean up underneath your bed
than read a paper you do not want to read.

00:37:15.940 --> 00:37:20.170
You cannot just tell yourself, well, I need
to read this paper for the following reasons,

00:37:20.170 --> 00:37:22.390
and proof, it becomes interesting.

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:27.710
No no, your value systems, whatever they happen
to be, are off doing their own thing.

00:37:27.710 --> 00:37:31.450
The reason for that, in large part, is because
they're possessed by ideas that you do not

00:37:31.450 --> 00:37:32.450
know about,

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:37.720
that have these historical roots and that
play you in some sense, like they play puppets

00:37:37.720 --> 00:37:39.070
and the stuff is no joke.

00:37:39.070 --> 00:37:42.990
Okay so this is what Dostoyevsky said, way
back in the late 1800s.

00:37:42.990 --> 00:37:49.820
This was in a book called "Notes from Underground",
and it is about a man who is like Hamlet.

00:37:49.820 --> 00:37:52.280
In some sense, he is a modern man.

00:37:52.280 --> 00:37:57.740
He's a 20th century man really and his problem
is, he is hyper- intelligent and he cannot

00:37:57.740 --> 00:37:59.970
figure out what the hell he should do with
his life.

00:37:59.970 --> 00:38:01.510
And it is really bothering him

00:38:01.510 --> 00:38:05.600
and it is worse than that, because not only
is it really bothering him, that he cannot

00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:10.230
get his act together, and act with any degree
of consistency in character,

00:38:10.230 --> 00:38:11.640
but he knows, that he cannot do that.

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:14.329
And he tortures himself about his weakness
at the same time.

00:38:14.329 --> 00:38:16.150
So he is a very neurotic character.

00:38:16.150 --> 00:38:19.089
But he is a sophisticated and intelligent
neurotic.

00:38:19.089 --> 00:38:23.631
And so he has run through all the arguments
that you might conjure up, to sort of, talk

00:38:23.631 --> 00:38:25.700
yourself out of being neurotic and suffering.

00:38:25.700 --> 00:38:28.119
He has nothing but contempt for his own character.

00:38:28.119 --> 00:38:33.310
He thinks he is much weaker than people, who
can just act without thinking.

00:38:33.310 --> 00:38:35.390
And he's in this pit, this horrible pit.

00:38:35.390 --> 00:38:37.220
And it is a wonderful thing to read.

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:43.619
It is quite blackly comical and it is a great
philosophical and psychological study.

00:38:43.619 --> 00:38:50.780
Anyways, in one of the sections of this book,
Dostoyevsky's protagonist, starts to talk

00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:57.340
about alternatives to his nihilistic hopelessness.
and he thinks about utopianism as a potential

00:38:57.340 --> 00:38:58.340
alternative.

00:38:58.340 --> 00:39:01.569
So what is utopianism?

00:39:01.569 --> 00:39:10.460
Well, in some sense, medieval Christianity
promised people redemption after they died.

00:39:10.460 --> 00:39:18.000
While a utopian creed does the same thing,
except it promises it, here and now.

00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:22.079
Communism was a particular utopian creed and
fascism had the same element.

00:39:22.079 --> 00:39:33.119
Although it was, I do not know how to describe,
it was less intellectually sophisticated than

00:39:33.119 --> 00:39:34.119
communism.

00:39:34.119 --> 00:39:40.050
Communists basically said look, if you guys
just stop being selfish and share, we can

00:39:40.050 --> 00:39:44.460
transform the world into a place where everyone
will have enough of everything, and everyone

00:39:44.460 --> 00:39:47.240
will be able to do what they want to do.

00:39:47.240 --> 00:39:52.860
And because of the natural goodness of people,
if selfishness can be overcome, that will

00:39:52.860 --> 00:39:55.690
be the next best thing to a paradise.

00:39:55.690 --> 00:40:00.290
It was a powerful idea for people, you know?

00:40:00.290 --> 00:40:07.840
80 years of our history was spent assessing
and battling out the validity of that idea.

00:40:07.840 --> 00:40:12.520
Hundreds of millions of people died as a consequence
of it.

00:40:12.520 --> 00:40:14.250
And you can understand why it was so attractive.

00:40:14.250 --> 00:40:20.490
I mean, still utopian ideologies are attractive
to people and it is hard to read radical Islam

00:40:20.490 --> 00:40:23.190
as anything other than a utopian ideology.

00:40:23.190 --> 00:40:29.280
You know, the idea is, once you establish
rigid sharia, then poof, you know, you got

00:40:29.280 --> 00:40:32.420
the kingdom of God on earth.

00:40:32.420 --> 00:40:37.359
And part of the reason that the radical Muslims
are fighting against the West, is because

00:40:37.359 --> 00:40:43.190
they see what they are doing as a counter
position to Western nihilism.

00:40:43.190 --> 00:40:46.670
And is partly because they do not want to
fall into that, you know.

00:40:46.670 --> 00:40:48.980
We would say well that is progress.

00:40:48.980 --> 00:40:54.250
Yeah, it is progress, by our standards, and
it comes at a price.

00:40:54.250 --> 00:40:58.390
and also we do not even understand how it
was that we paid the price.

00:40:58.390 --> 00:41:04.450
So the reason I am telling you this, is because,
you do not be thinking, for any time at all,

00:41:04.450 --> 00:41:08.230
that these sorts of issues have disappeared
or that they are not relevant.

00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:09.460
They're relevant.

00:41:09.460 --> 00:41:12.130
Now the guy who is advising Putin.

00:41:12.130 --> 00:41:16.930
His name is Alexander Dugan and he is no admirer
of Western liberalism.

00:41:16.930 --> 00:41:19.200
He thinks about it as fundamentally nihilistic.

00:41:19.200 --> 00:41:25.740
He thinks that its universal application would
result in the dissolution of all local culture

00:41:25.740 --> 00:41:32.120
and the production of this sort of materialistic
hyper- individuality.

00:41:32.120 --> 00:41:34.839
He's an admirer of tradition.

00:41:34.839 --> 00:41:37.980
And you know, specifically Russian Orthodox
tradition.

00:41:37.980 --> 00:41:42.920
And he believes that the cultures, India,
Russia and China in particular, should develop

00:41:42.920 --> 00:41:48.079
their own local cultures, keep the West the
hell out, and act as a counter position to

00:41:48.079 --> 00:41:49.980
nihilistic liberalism.

00:41:49.980 --> 00:41:54.390
Now you know, you can say what you want about
that.

00:41:54.390 --> 00:42:00.910
I think Dugan's biggest problem is that, you
know, he does not want that the diverse ideas

00:42:00.910 --> 00:42:05.359
that characterize the West to bump up against
Russia and dissolve it.

00:42:05.359 --> 00:42:10.530
But what he fails to understand is those same
ideas are going to emerge within Russia, anyways

00:42:10.530 --> 00:42:15.050
and if you know, if you want to keep them
away outside, you have to keep them away inside

00:42:15.050 --> 00:42:20.170
and the Soviets already tried that for 70
years with pretty dire results.

00:42:20.170 --> 00:42:27.750
So I don't think he can get around the problem
merely by putting up walls, but he is going

00:42:27.750 --> 00:42:29.250
to try.

00:42:29.250 --> 00:42:31.000
And that is what Putin is doing.

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:33.329
So these ideas haven't disappeared at all.

00:42:33.329 --> 00:42:38.310
They underlie all of the great conflicts that
characterize the modern age.

00:42:38.310 --> 00:42:44.660
Dostoyevsky criticized utopianism and it its
brilliant, his formulation, so I am going

00:42:44.660 --> 00:42:46.480
to read it to you.

00:42:46.480 --> 00:42:51.200
In short, one may say anything about the history
of the world.

00:42:51.200 --> 00:42:54.619
Anything that might enter the most disordered
imagination.

00:42:54.619 --> 00:42:57.710
The only thing one can say is that it is rational.

00:42:57.710 --> 00:43:03.460
The very word sticks in one's throat, and
indeed, this is the odd thing that is continually

00:43:03.460 --> 00:43:04.460
happening.

00:43:04.460 --> 00:43:09.700
They're continually turning up in life, moral
and rational persons, sages and lovers of

00:43:09.700 --> 00:43:13.829
humanity, who make it their object to live
all their lives as morally and rationally

00:43:13.829 --> 00:43:15.050
as possible.

00:43:15.050 --> 00:43:20.030
To be, so to speak, alike to their neighbours,
simply in order to show them that it is possible

00:43:20.030 --> 00:43:22.810
to live morally and rationally in this world.

00:43:22.810 --> 00:43:26.460
And yet we all know that these very people,
soon or later, have been false to themselves

00:43:26.460 --> 00:43:32.599
playing some queer trick, often a most unseemly
one.

00:43:32.599 --> 00:43:38.420
Now I ask you: what can be expected of man
since he is a being endowed with such strange

00:43:38.420 --> 00:43:39.420
qualities?

00:43:39.420 --> 00:43:42.690
Shower upon him every earthly blessing.

00:43:42.690 --> 00:43:47.820
Drown him in a sea of happiness, so that nothing
but bubbles of bliss can be seen on the surface.

00:43:47.820 --> 00:43:52.640
Give him economic prosperity, such that he
should have nothing else to do but sleep,

00:43:52.640 --> 00:43:58.000
eat cakes and busy himself with the continuation
of his species.

00:43:58.000 --> 00:44:03.810
And even then out of sheer ingratitude, sheer
spite, man would play you some nasty trick.

00:44:03.810 --> 00:44:10.460
He would even risk his cakes and would deliberately
desire the most fatal rubbish, the most uneconomical

00:44:10.460 --> 00:44:17.000
absurdity, simply to introduce into all this
positive good sense, his fatal fantastic element.

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:22.520
It is just his fantastic dreams, his vulgar
folly that he will desire to retain, simply

00:44:22.520 --> 00:44:27.579
in order to prove to himself, as though that
were so necessary,

00:44:27.579 --> 00:44:32.300
that men still are men, and not the keys of
a piano, which the laws of nature threaten

00:44:32.300 --> 00:44:36.859
to control completely, so completely, that
one will be able to desire nothing but by

00:44:36.859 --> 00:44:39.210
the calendar.

00:44:39.210 --> 00:44:45.180
Clearly that is Dostoyevsky's criticism of
materialistic determinism, which he felt as

00:44:45.180 --> 00:44:52.369
a spiritual threat fundamentally, its proposition
being that animals and human beings were deterministic

00:44:52.369 --> 00:44:53.369
machines.

00:44:53.369 --> 00:44:57.940
It is a Newtonian worldview and because of
that, everything could be calculated and planned

00:44:57.940 --> 00:45:04.970
ahead of time, because it could be predicted
and measured, and that is not all.

00:45:04.970 --> 00:45:09.700
Even if man really were nothing but a piano
key, even if this were proved to him by natural

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:14.130
science and mathematics, even then he would
not become reasonable, but would purposely

00:45:14.130 --> 00:45:19.079
do something perverse out of simple ingratitude,
simply to gain his point.

00:45:19.079 --> 00:45:25.080
And if he does not find means, he will contrive
destruction and chaos, sufferings of all sorts,

00:45:25.080 --> 00:45:26.810
only to gain his point.

00:45:26.810 --> 00:45:33.119
He will launch a curse upon the world, and
as only man can curse, it is his privilege,

00:45:33.119 --> 00:45:37.230
and the primary distinction between him and
other animals, maybe by his curse alone he

00:45:37.230 --> 00:45:42.830
will attain his object and convince himself
that he is a man and not a piano key.

00:45:42.830 --> 00:45:48.150
If you say that all this, too, can be calculated
and tabulated, chaos and darkness and curses,

00:45:48.150 --> 00:45:53.550
so that the mere possibility of calculating
it all beforehand would stop it all, and reason

00:45:53.550 --> 00:45:57.980
would reassert itself, then man would purposely
go mad in order to be rid of reason and gain

00:45:57.980 --> 00:45:59.000
his point.

00:45:59.000 --> 00:46:00.640
I believe in it, I answer for it.

00:46:00.640 --> 00:46:04.569
For the whole work of man really seems to
consist in nothing but proving to himself

00:46:04.569 --> 00:46:08.620
every minute that he is a man and not a piano
key.

00:46:08.620 --> 00:46:11.230
It may be at the cost of his skin.

00:46:11.230 --> 00:46:14.640
It might be by cannibalism.

00:46:14.640 --> 00:46:19.849
And this being so, can one help being tempted
to rejoice that it has not yet come off, and

00:46:19.849 --> 00:46:23.800
that desire still depends on something we
don't know?

00:46:23.800 --> 00:46:27.440
You will scream at me, that is, if you condescend
to do so.

00:46:27.440 --> 00:46:31.470
That no one is touching my free will, that
all they're concerned with is that my will

00:46:31.470 --> 00:46:38.339
should of, should of itself, of its own free
will, coincide with my own normal interests,

00:46:38.339 --> 00:46:41.240
with the laws of nature, and arithmetic.

00:46:41.240 --> 00:46:45.150
Good heavens gentleman, what sort of free
will is left when we come to tabulation and

00:46:45.150 --> 00:46:46.150
arithmetic.

00:46:46.150 --> 00:46:50.619
When it will all be a case of twice two makes
four.

00:46:50.619 --> 00:46:52.869
Twice two makes four without my will.

00:46:52.869 --> 00:46:58.040
As if free will meant that.

00:46:58.040 --> 00:47:00.190
So what's his point?

00:47:00.190 --> 00:47:03.970
It's sort of a Garden of Eden point, you know?

00:47:03.970 --> 00:47:05.619
What are people like?

00:47:05.619 --> 00:47:10.110
Imagine you can reconstruct a paradise on
earth?

00:47:10.110 --> 00:47:11.110
You know?

00:47:11.110 --> 00:47:12.110
Hypothetically that's what everyone wants.

00:47:12.110 --> 00:47:15.030
We can go live in a paradise, and that would
be the end of the problem.

00:47:15.030 --> 00:47:16.670
We'd all live happily ever after.

00:47:16.670 --> 00:47:21.640
But in the original paradise story, that's
what people were provided with.

00:47:21.640 --> 00:47:25.130
And the first thing they did when they were
put there, was to do the one thing that they

00:47:25.130 --> 00:47:27.800
were told not to do, that would bring it all
crashing down.

00:47:27.800 --> 00:47:30.230
And that was immediately what they did.

00:47:30.230 --> 00:47:33.900
And so Dostoyevsky's stories are actually
a retelling of that idea.

00:47:33.900 --> 00:47:38.720
The idea was that people aren't like the utopians
think.

00:47:38.720 --> 00:47:39.829
We don't want it easy.

00:47:39.829 --> 00:47:42.110
We don't want it comfortable.

00:47:42.110 --> 00:47:43.110
We don't want it good.

00:47:43.110 --> 00:47:45.650
And the reason for that is, we'd be bored
stiff.

00:47:45.650 --> 00:47:50.180
And so that if anybody ever did put us in
the kind of nursery, that would require us

00:47:50.180 --> 00:47:55.670
never to exert any effort to do anything at
all whatsoever ever again, even if it meant

00:47:55.670 --> 00:47:58.420
going insane, we'd destroy it.

00:47:58.420 --> 00:48:04.710
And then he takes that further, he says, and
that is a good thing.

00:48:04.710 --> 00:48:10.640
Kierkegaard writing earlier, about 40 years
earlier, said something quite similar.

00:48:10.640 --> 00:48:13.880
It is now about four years ago that I got
the notion of wanting to try my luck as an

00:48:13.880 --> 00:48:17.599
author I remember quite clearly, it was on
a Sunday.

00:48:17.599 --> 00:48:19.750
Yes, that is it, a Sunday afternoon.

00:48:19.750 --> 00:48:25.720
I was seated as usual, out-of-doors at the
caf� in the Fredericksburg garden.

00:48:25.720 --> 00:48:29.270
I had been a student for half a score of years.

00:48:29.270 --> 00:48:34.480
Although never lazy, all my activity, nevertheless
was like a glittering inactivity, a kind of

00:48:34.480 --> 00:48:39.410
occupation for which I still have great partiality
and for which, perhaps, I even have a little

00:48:39.410 --> 00:48:40.410
genius.

00:48:40.410 --> 00:48:46.050
I read much, spent the remainder of the day
idling and thinking, or thinking and idling,

00:48:46.050 --> 00:48:47.870
but that was all it came to.

00:48:47.870 --> 00:48:51.630
So there I sat and smoked my cigar until I
lapsed into thought.

00:48:51.630 --> 00:48:54.559
Among other thoughts I remember these.

00:48:54.559 --> 00:49:01.270
You are going on, I said to myself, to become
an old man without being anything and really

00:49:01.270 --> 00:49:04.140
without undertaking to do anything.

00:49:04.140 --> 00:49:07.340
On the other hand, wherever you look about
you in literature and in life, you see the

00:49:07.340 --> 00:49:12.180
celebrated names and figures, the precious
and much heralded men, who were coming into

00:49:12.180 --> 00:49:14.770
prominence and are much talked about.

00:49:14.770 --> 00:49:18.590
the many benefactors of the age who know how
to benefit mankind by making life easier and

00:49:18.590 --> 00:49:19.740
easier.

00:49:19.740 --> 00:49:24.990
Some by railways, others by omnibuses and
steamboats others by the telegraph, others

00:49:24.990 --> 00:49:29.430
quite easily apprehended compendiums and short
recitals of everything worth knowing.

00:49:29.430 --> 00:49:35.490
Finally, the true benefactors of the age make
spiritual existence in virtue of thought easier

00:49:35.490 --> 00:49:39.010
and easier yet more and more significant.

00:49:39.010 --> 00:49:40.829
And what are you doing?

00:49:40.829 --> 00:49:48.329
Here my soliloquy was interrupted for my cigar
was smoked out and a new one had to be lit.

00:49:48.329 --> 00:49:53.559
So I smoked again, and then suddenly this
thought flashed through my mind.

00:49:53.559 --> 00:49:58.460
You must do something, but inasmuch as with
your limited capacities will be impossible

00:49:58.460 --> 00:50:01.530
to make anything easier than it has become.

00:50:01.530 --> 00:50:06.990
You must, with the same humanitarian enthusiasm
as the others, undertake to make something

00:50:06.990 --> 00:50:08.040
harder.

00:50:08.040 --> 00:50:12.700
This notion pleased me immensely and at the
same time, it flattered me to think that I,

00:50:12.700 --> 00:50:16.770
like the rest of them, would be loved and
esteemed by the whole community.

00:50:16.770 --> 00:50:21.610
For when all combine in every way to make
everything easier, there remains only one

00:50:21.610 --> 00:50:23.450
possible danger.

00:50:23.450 --> 00:50:28.339
Namely, that the ease become so great, that
it becomes altogether too great.

00:50:28.339 --> 00:50:32.250
Then there is only one want left, though it
is not yet felt want.

00:50:32.250 --> 00:50:34.180
When people will want difficulty.

00:50:34.180 --> 00:50:39.560
Out of love for mankind and out of despair
at my embarrassing situation, seeing that

00:50:39.560 --> 00:50:41.160
I had accomplished nothing,

00:50:41.160 --> 00:50:45.599
and was unable to make anything easier than
it already been made, and moved by a genuine

00:50:45.599 --> 00:50:51.849
interest in those who make everything easy,
I conceived it as my task to create difficulties,

00:50:51.849 --> 00:50:53.530
everywhere.

00:50:53.530 --> 00:51:02.460
Now, one of the things you might ask yourself
is, sometimes you come to University and people

00:51:02.460 --> 00:51:03.970
talk about happiness.

00:51:03.970 --> 00:51:06.079
For example, they talk about positive psychology.

00:51:06.079 --> 00:51:10.210
I am not a fan of positive psychology, by
the way.

00:51:10.210 --> 00:51:15.150
Because happiness is basically extraversion
minus neuroticism and we knew that 15 years

00:51:15.150 --> 00:51:16.150
ago.

00:51:16.150 --> 00:51:18.950
So we didn't need to make a lot of noise about
it.

00:51:18.950 --> 00:51:25.260
So anyways, one of the things you might ask
yourself is, well, why did you come to University?

00:51:25.260 --> 00:51:29.210
Did you actually come to University to make
yourself happier?

00:51:29.210 --> 00:51:35.040
Well, let's think about that for a minute.

00:51:35.040 --> 00:51:37.960
Here's one thing to think about?

00:51:37.960 --> 00:51:42.900
We know that if you put animals in a relatively
boring situation, like rats, in a boring situation

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:47.670
and you give them free access to cocaine,
they'll just take cocaine until they are dead

00:51:47.670 --> 00:51:48.670
basically.

00:51:48.670 --> 00:51:53.349
Now rats in a normal environment won't do
that, but bored rats that are sort of isolated,

00:51:53.349 --> 00:51:55.770
cocaine is really an excellent thing, as far
as they are concerned.

00:51:55.770 --> 00:51:57.630
They'll ignore sex, they'll ignore food.

00:51:57.630 --> 00:52:00.599
I think they'll still drink water, if I remember
correctly.

00:52:00.599 --> 00:52:02.960
But it is cocaine all the way.

00:52:02.960 --> 00:52:07.299
And if you could inject an electrode into
their minds, their brains, which people have

00:52:07.299 --> 00:52:13.630
done, you can inject the electrode down into
the hypothalamus, into the part that is associated

00:52:13.630 --> 00:52:15.000
with the reward centres.

00:52:15.000 --> 00:52:20.140
It is the source of the dopaminergic tract
and you can set them up so that if the rat

00:52:20.140 --> 00:52:24.960
pushes a button, they get a jolt of happiness,
basically, and the rat will sit there and

00:52:24.960 --> 00:52:32.230
push that happiness button in a rather frantic
way, as if it is looking for something else,

00:52:32.230 --> 00:52:33.230
in some sense.

00:52:33.230 --> 00:52:36.450
But it will certainly do it, because it is
a peculiar kind of reward.

00:52:36.450 --> 00:52:43.050
Now the question might be, would you allow
yourself to be wired up like that?

00:52:43.050 --> 00:52:45.980
Now you might think there might be some times
in your life where you think that might just

00:52:45.980 --> 00:52:51.690
be perfectly fine idea, but most of you, I
suppose, I presume, wouldn't do that for second,

00:52:51.690 --> 00:52:56.040
for the same reasons, perhaps, that you do
not avail yourself of unlimited access to

00:52:56.040 --> 00:52:57.040
cocaine.

00:52:57.040 --> 00:53:00.600
Which is a stimulant that is very good at
producing positive emotion.

00:53:00.600 --> 00:53:05.200
It is a powerful psychomotor stimulant and
so it affects the parts of your brain that

00:53:05.200 --> 00:53:09.700
are active when you are doing something that
you think is worthwhile and productive.

00:53:09.700 --> 00:53:10.910
So why not just do that all the time?

00:53:10.910 --> 00:53:14.870
That is the question that Aldous Huxley asked
in Brave New World.

00:53:14.870 --> 00:53:21.240
You've got everything you want, take a drug
to keep you calm and happy, poof; perfect.

00:53:21.240 --> 00:53:23.839
Well, is that what you want?

00:53:23.839 --> 00:53:28.390
And if answer is no, then you might ask yourself,
what the hell do you want?

00:53:28.390 --> 00:53:35.670
You know, one of the things I thought a lot
about lately is, lately being 10 years, I

00:53:35.670 --> 00:53:36.760
suppose is

00:53:36.760 --> 00:53:38.430
there are these statues that I've seen.

00:53:38.430 --> 00:53:39.859
I've looked at pictures of them online.

00:53:39.859 --> 00:53:44.890
There are statues of Atlas, and you know Atlas,
he's this God who has the world on his shoulders.

00:53:44.890 --> 00:53:49.150
And that is his destiny or his curse, to have
the world on his shoulders.

00:53:49.150 --> 00:53:51.460
And he might say, well, you know, poor Atlas.

00:53:51.460 --> 00:53:56.140
Maybe he should just put the damn world down
and you know, go out for a beer or something.

00:53:56.140 --> 00:54:03.839
But then you might also think, well what is
that figure trying to, what is that that idea

00:54:03.839 --> 00:54:06.340
trying to indicate cause it's an old idea.

00:54:06.340 --> 00:54:08.140
It is a profound idea.

00:54:08.140 --> 00:54:11.609
There's something divine about a figure with
the world on its shoulders.

00:54:11.609 --> 00:54:15.359
Well, I might say that's the reason you are
in University whether you know it or not.

00:54:15.359 --> 00:54:22.540
You are here to take the world on your shoulders,
because that is a sufficiently profound and

00:54:22.540 --> 00:54:29.339
worthwhile exercise, so that all the suffering
that you are going to have, might be regarded

00:54:29.339 --> 00:54:30.930
as worthwhile.

00:54:30.930 --> 00:54:34.010
Because the value of what you are doing is
so high, because that is something you might

00:54:34.010 --> 00:54:40.240
ask, is there something that you could do,
whose value is so high, that the fact,

00:54:40.240 --> 00:54:47.299
for the existentialists, it's a fact, that
you're mortal and vulnerable and prone to

00:54:47.299 --> 00:54:50.030
suffering, inescapably.

00:54:50.030 --> 00:54:53.010
That you would find that not only acceptable,
but desirable.

00:54:53.010 --> 00:54:58.390
You might say that you would pay that price.

00:54:58.390 --> 00:55:01.770
You might say that is the existential question.

00:55:01.770 --> 00:55:04.630
And one of the things it is very interesting
about that question.

00:55:04.630 --> 00:55:08.770
I am going to talk about this a lot next class.

00:55:08.770 --> 00:55:14.160
What happens if you make the opposite choice?

00:55:14.160 --> 00:55:17.690
I think the 20th century actually showed us
what happened when people made the opposite

00:55:17.690 --> 00:55:19.609
choice.

00:55:19.609 --> 00:55:28.309
Because as far as I can tell, when people
abandon their Divine responsibility, let us

00:55:28.309 --> 00:55:35.660
say, to the utopian claims of a totalitarian
state, or to hopeless nihilism,

00:55:35.660 --> 00:55:42.849
the consequence on the one hand, with nihilism
was despair and illness, and the consequence

00:55:42.849 --> 00:55:48.049
on the totalitarian end of things, the utopian
end was that you might not die, but you are

00:55:48.049 --> 00:55:52.960
certainly going to have a hand in making sure
that a lot of other people do.

00:55:52.960 --> 00:56:04.020
And so, to some degree it depends on what
you want for proof with regards to what you

00:56:04.020 --> 00:56:05.349
should do.

00:56:05.349 --> 00:56:10.890
Now, the conclusion I have drawn from all
this, from reading the existentialist is that,

00:56:10.890 --> 00:56:19.190
if it is the pointless suffering of humanity
and the inability to extract meaning from

00:56:19.190 --> 00:56:22.880
that, that makes you a nihilist and that justifies
it, let us say.

00:56:22.880 --> 00:56:24.440
You are making a claim right?

00:56:24.440 --> 00:56:30.859
The claim is, the implicit claim is, that
suffering is bad and should be halted.

00:56:30.859 --> 00:56:32.280
It is something like that.

00:56:32.280 --> 00:56:36.670
And so maybe you will do that by becoming
suicidal or maybe you do that by becoming

00:56:36.670 --> 00:56:42.400
ultimately genocidal, which is also an option
that's open to more than a few people.

00:56:42.400 --> 00:56:49.030
But there is a logical inconsistency in that
as far as I can tell which is that your initial

00:56:49.030 --> 00:56:51.859
presupposition is that the suffering is actually
bad.

00:56:51.859 --> 00:56:52.859
It should be mitigated.

00:56:52.859 --> 00:56:54.710
That should be it should be reduced.

00:56:54.710 --> 00:56:57.660
It should perhaps even be eliminated.

00:56:57.660 --> 00:57:03.220
If you pick up the cloak of nihilism, or maybe
you pick up the cloak of ideological totalitarianism,

00:57:03.220 --> 00:57:06.849
then we know what the consequence of that
is.

00:57:06.849 --> 00:57:11.320
The consequence is that everything that is
already really bad, becomes so much worse,

00:57:11.320 --> 00:57:14.740
that it is almost unimaginable.

00:57:14.740 --> 00:57:20.859
And so, even by the standards of the nihilist,
who says that the suffering of being should

00:57:20.859 --> 00:57:24.910
result in its elimination.

00:57:24.910 --> 00:57:28.930
The consequences of thinking that way, or
flipping to the other side and adopting some

00:57:28.930 --> 00:57:34.460
sort of defensive ideology is that, things
go from being, you know, merely the sort of

00:57:34.460 --> 00:57:37.280
bad state of the earth as it is now,

00:57:37.280 --> 00:57:42.510
to something as hellish as the Soviet gulags,
or the Russian concentration camps or Mao's

00:57:42.510 --> 00:57:46.970
great experiment in the cultural Revolution,
which probably killed 100 million people.

00:57:46.970 --> 00:57:52.640
You know, about which we generally hear nothing.

00:57:52.640 --> 00:57:59.160
Now you know one of the things the existentialist
would say is, what is the relationship between

00:57:59.160 --> 00:58:01.140
mental health and responsibility?

00:58:01.140 --> 00:58:07.960
Now that's a good question because it also
has to do with something like the definition

00:58:07.960 --> 00:58:10.740
of mental health and responsibility.

00:58:10.740 --> 00:58:18.829
It is like, if you want your life to be well
constituted, let us say, whatever that means.

00:58:18.829 --> 00:58:22.460
And it does not mean being happy.

00:58:22.460 --> 00:58:25.820
The reason it can't mean being happy is there's
going to be times in your life or you're to

00:58:25.820 --> 00:58:29.450
be called on to act, when you are not happy.

00:58:29.450 --> 00:58:34.940
So, for example, when one of your parents
dies, you're going to make a choice.

00:58:34.940 --> 00:58:36.579
You are not going to be happy.

00:58:36.579 --> 00:58:38.670
Hopefully, you won't be.

00:58:38.670 --> 00:58:40.550
Otherwise you are tangled in some sort of
Freudian nightmare.

00:58:40.550 --> 00:58:42.750
But let us assume that that is not the case.

00:58:42.750 --> 00:58:44.390
You are not going to be happy about it.

00:58:44.390 --> 00:58:47.940
You're going to be hurt and maybe even partly
broken.

00:58:47.940 --> 00:58:50.960
So what the hell you supposed to do, then?

00:58:50.960 --> 00:58:53.530
Well the answer is, you should be more use
than trouble.

00:58:53.530 --> 00:58:58.210
Under some such circumstances, that's a good
thing to strive for, you know?

00:58:58.210 --> 00:58:59.799
Because your mother would be, if it is your
father.

00:58:59.799 --> 00:59:03.309
And your mother's to be equally distraught
and so are your siblings and everyone else

00:59:03.309 --> 00:59:04.420
you care for.

00:59:04.420 --> 00:59:08.430
Maybe by that time, you should be tough enough
so that in that situation, you are good for

00:59:08.430 --> 00:59:09.500
something.

00:59:09.500 --> 00:59:12.330
Someone has to make the damn funeral arrangements.

00:59:12.330 --> 00:59:14.430
Someone has to settle out the will.

00:59:14.430 --> 00:59:18.980
Someone has to make sure the family does not
degenerate into horrific squabbling, which

00:59:18.980 --> 00:59:23.040
is something that often happens after the
death of a parent.

00:59:23.040 --> 00:59:25.849
And you are not going to be motivated to do
that by happiness.

00:59:25.849 --> 00:59:29.880
And what if you have a sick child, when you
are a parent.

00:59:29.880 --> 00:59:31.099
Maybe it is a chronic illness.

00:59:31.099 --> 00:59:33.270
You are not going to be happy about that.

00:59:33.270 --> 00:59:38.160
You know it will be a weight that you carry
with you all the time and it is one of those

00:59:38.160 --> 00:59:39.160
things.

00:59:39.160 --> 00:59:40.850
It seems particularly unjust.

00:59:40.850 --> 00:59:43.630
Then, you are no longer happy.

00:59:43.630 --> 00:59:47.819
If being happy is the purpose of life, then
you are basically, that is pretty much it

00:59:47.819 --> 00:59:49.559
for you.

00:59:49.559 --> 00:59:53.980
And you know these catastrophes that I am
speaking of.

00:59:53.980 --> 00:59:59.590
You can be certain that you are going to be
exposed to many of those during your life.

00:59:59.590 --> 01:00:00.730
You know.

01:00:00.730 --> 01:00:06.130
It will be a rare period, I think it is rare,
in anyone's life where one or more of such

01:00:06.130 --> 01:00:08.359
things is not going on chronically.

01:00:08.359 --> 01:00:13.579
It is not you, with some terrible health problem,
or some other terrible problem, then it is

01:00:13.579 --> 01:00:16.160
a parent or a sibling or a child.

01:00:16.160 --> 01:00:19.570
Because you know, you are connected to other
people and their vulnerable to so that is

01:00:19.570 --> 01:00:21.670
the law of human beings.

01:00:21.670 --> 01:00:25.710
And so if it is happiness and this is what
Solzhenitsyn said about happiness too.

01:00:25.710 --> 01:00:33.520
He said happiness is a philosophy, who's brought
to ruin by the first blow of a guard's truncheon.

01:00:33.520 --> 01:00:42.319
Like yeah, that is about as bluntly as you
can put it.

01:00:42.319 --> 01:00:53.720
Here is another, I have time to read both
of these.

01:00:53.720 --> 01:00:57.730
I think this is from Kierkegaard as well.

01:00:57.730 --> 01:01:03.900
Kierkegaard, by the way, was really the first
thinker who identified what we would describe

01:01:03.900 --> 01:01:09.869
in modern terms as anxiety, especially as
existential anxiety or angst.

01:01:09.869 --> 01:01:14.730
And it would be associated with the condition
of questioning the nature of existence, the

01:01:14.730 --> 01:01:18.930
utility of existence, and so Kierkegaard was
really the first person who formalized that

01:01:18.930 --> 01:01:23.869
into something resembling a philosophy or
a psychology

01:01:23.869 --> 01:01:26.800
and that he was trying to think about how
that might be overcome.

01:01:26.800 --> 01:01:30.920
Given that it seemed to be rooted in fact,
the factual observation.

01:01:30.920 --> 01:01:34.700
That is the observation of suffering

01:01:34.700 --> 01:01:40.510
and this is a corollary to Dostoyevsky's comments
even though Kierkegaard's comments were written

01:01:40.510 --> 01:01:42.460
decades earlier.

01:01:42.460 --> 01:01:51.020
Dostoyevsky's critique basically said, you
cannot solve the problem of suffering by formalizing

01:01:51.020 --> 01:01:54.619
a utopia and then enjoining it like a mass
animal.

01:01:54.619 --> 01:01:57.160
You cannot do that because you are not that
kind of creature.

01:01:57.160 --> 01:02:01.570
Even if it was possible, you would not accept
it, you cause trouble because you are interested

01:02:01.570 --> 01:02:02.570
in trouble.

01:02:02.570 --> 01:02:07.059
You are probably more interested in trouble
than you are at being happy.

01:02:07.059 --> 01:02:09.000
So I mean, you know people like that.

01:02:09.000 --> 01:02:13.220
That is another marker of serious personality
disorder, right?

01:02:13.220 --> 01:02:18.849
I have clients, have many of them, who are
way more interested in causing trouble in

01:02:18.849 --> 01:02:22.470
some dramatic way than they are in being boring
and stable.

01:02:22.470 --> 01:02:27.500
They will take any form of suffering and inflict
any form of suffering on any number of people

01:02:27.500 --> 01:02:32.740
they can possibly get their hooks into in
order merely to escape, you know, drab and

01:02:32.740 --> 01:02:34.410
secure normality.

01:02:34.410 --> 01:02:38.450
You know, you call those people dramatic,
overly dramatic.

01:02:38.450 --> 01:02:42.150
That is one way of looking at it.

01:02:42.150 --> 01:02:45.910
And they make, as far as I can tell, a relatively
conscious choice.

01:02:45.910 --> 01:02:52.510
Trouble is more interesting than safety.

01:02:52.510 --> 01:02:55.819
Kierkegaard says something similar.

01:02:55.819 --> 01:03:04.170
But in 
a manner that is more constructive, in some

01:03:04.170 --> 01:03:08.400
sense, with regards to what you should do
with all that insane energy that you are not

01:03:08.400 --> 01:03:12.819
going to be able to encapsulate inside a utopia.

01:03:12.819 --> 01:03:16.670
And it has to do with individual responsibility.

01:03:16.670 --> 01:03:20.960
There is a view of life, which conceives that
where the crowd is, there is also the truth.

01:03:20.960 --> 01:03:25.380
And then, in truth itself, there is need of
having the crowd on its side.

01:03:25.380 --> 01:03:27.490
I was on a panel at one point about.

01:03:27.490 --> 01:03:34.900
I think we were discussing gender differences
between, obviously between men and women and

01:03:34.900 --> 01:03:35.920
there are lots of people.

01:03:35.920 --> 01:03:39.520
The social constructionists, in particular,
who think that all the differences; there

01:03:39.520 --> 01:03:44.730
is biological sex and then there is gender
and gender is socially constructed and that

01:03:44.730 --> 01:03:46.420
all gender differences are socially constructed.

01:03:46.420 --> 01:03:51.260
And there is no biological differences in
gender between the two sexes.

01:03:51.260 --> 01:03:54.770
Now, virtually no evidence supports that proposition.

01:03:54.770 --> 01:03:58.660
If you look at the hard-core psychological
evidence, in fact, it is completely the opposite.

01:03:58.660 --> 01:04:04.780
And not only that, as you make societies more
egalitarian, men and women get more different,

01:04:04.780 --> 01:04:06.630
instead of more the same.

01:04:06.630 --> 01:04:11.710
Now the reason that happens is because, once
you iron out the environmental variability,

01:04:11.710 --> 01:04:16.280
by equalizing everything, all that is left
is genetic variability.

01:04:16.280 --> 01:04:17.779
So it springs to the forefront.

01:04:17.779 --> 01:04:23.420
And so the biggest gender differences in the
world are between men and women in Scandinavia.

01:04:23.420 --> 01:04:26.060
and those are partly personality differences.

01:04:26.060 --> 01:04:30.550
Women are higher in negative emotion and more
agreeable, among other things, but more particularly,

01:04:30.550 --> 01:04:34.619
the differences seem to be those of interest.

01:04:34.619 --> 01:04:38.569
So the biggest differences between men and
women seem to be, what they are interested

01:04:38.569 --> 01:04:39.569
in.

01:04:39.569 --> 01:04:43.160
and roughly speaking, women are more interested
in people and roughly speaking, men are more

01:04:43.160 --> 01:04:44.160
interested in things.

01:04:44.160 --> 01:04:49.529
And so in Scandinavia, for example, you have
20 to 1 proportion of women to men in nursing,

01:04:49.529 --> 01:04:54.110
and a 20 to 1 proportion of men to women in
engineering.

01:04:54.110 --> 01:04:58.599
And so, you know, the Scandinavia governments,
now and then, try to move that, so there are

01:04:58.599 --> 01:05:00.329
more male nurses and more female engineers.

01:05:00.329 --> 01:05:05.170
And if they really push, they can move the
ratios somewhat, for a few years.

01:05:05.170 --> 01:05:09.360
But as they relax, they snap right back to
20 to 1.

01:05:09.360 --> 01:05:14.369
So anyways, I was sighting some of these studies,
and one of the people that I was discussing

01:05:14.369 --> 01:05:19.290
said, what are we supposed to do with that?

01:05:19.290 --> 01:05:21.279
And I said, I do not know what you mean.

01:05:21.279 --> 01:05:22.970
Those are scientific findings.

01:05:22.970 --> 01:05:26.460
He said that, yeah, truth has to be established
by consensus.

01:05:26.460 --> 01:05:32.569
And I thought I do not want to live in whatever
world you are going to end up ruling, because

01:05:32.569 --> 01:05:35.319
truth is not merely established by consensus.

01:05:35.319 --> 01:05:36.420
or if you think it does.

01:05:36.420 --> 01:05:40.150
If you think it is, then while you are in
a position that Kierkegaard describes, which

01:05:40.150 --> 01:05:44.289
is that, as long as everyone else believes
it, then the appropriate thing is for you

01:05:44.289 --> 01:05:46.490
to believe it, and also that is the truth.

01:05:46.490 --> 01:05:51.760
Like, it is a pretty damn dismal philosophy
and it gets people into tremendous trouble

01:05:51.760 --> 01:05:55.990
because no matter how many people think there
isn't a wall there, anyone who runs at it

01:05:55.990 --> 01:05:58.540
head first, is in for a vicious surprise.

01:05:58.540 --> 01:06:02.960
There is a view of life, which conceives that
where the crowd is, there is also the truth

01:06:02.960 --> 01:06:05.670
and in that truth, is in truth itself.

01:06:05.670 --> 01:06:07.549
There is need of having a crowd on its side.

01:06:07.549 --> 01:06:15.190
There is a view of life, which conceives that
wherever there is a crowd there is untruth.

01:06:15.190 --> 01:06:19.400
So that to consider for a moment, the extreme
case.

01:06:19.400 --> 01:06:25.180
Even if every individual, each for himself
in private, were to be in possession of the

01:06:25.180 --> 01:06:26.180
truth.

01:06:26.180 --> 01:06:30.880
Yet, in case they are all to get together
in a crowd, a crowd to which any sort of decisive

01:06:30.880 --> 01:06:36.569
significance is attributed; avoiding, voting,
noisy, audible crowd, untruth would immediately

01:06:36.569 --> 01:06:40.330
be in evidence.

01:06:40.330 --> 01:06:43.370
For a crowd is the untruth.

01:06:43.370 --> 01:06:48.579
In a godly sense, it is true, eternally, christianly,
as St. Paul says that only one attains the

01:06:48.579 --> 01:06:49.579
goal.

01:06:49.579 --> 01:06:51.280
Which is not meant in a comparative sense.

01:06:51.280 --> 01:06:53.950
For comparison takes others into account.

01:06:53.950 --> 01:06:56.880
It means that every individual can be that
one.

01:06:56.880 --> 01:06:59.609
God helping them therein, but only one attains
the goal.

01:06:59.609 --> 01:07:04.900
And again, this means that every man should
be careful about having to do with the others.

01:07:04.900 --> 01:07:08.020
And essentially should only talk with God
himself.

01:07:08.020 --> 01:07:10.329
For o
nly one attains the goal. and again this means

01:07:10.329 --> 01:07:14.240
that man or to be a man is akin to deity.

01:07:14.240 --> 01:07:19.000
In a worldly and temporal sense, it will be
said by the man of bustle, sociability and

01:07:19.000 --> 01:07:24.309
amicableness, how unreasonable, that only
one attains the goal.

01:07:24.309 --> 01:07:28.910
For is far more likely that many, by the strength
of united effort, should attain the goal.

01:07:28.910 --> 01:07:33.490
And when we were many success is more certain,
and it is easier for each man severally.

01:07:33.490 --> 01:07:38.589
True enough, it is far more likely, and it
is also true with respect to all earthly and

01:07:38.589 --> 01:07:39.670
material goods.

01:07:39.670 --> 01:07:42.270
If it is allowed to have its way.

01:07:42.270 --> 01:07:45.470
However, this becomes the only true point
of view.

01:07:45.470 --> 01:07:48.990
For it does away with God in eternity and
with man's kinship with deity.

01:07:48.990 --> 01:07:52.869
It does away with it, or transforms it into
a fable.

01:07:52.869 --> 01:07:57.480
And puts in its place, the modern, or we might
say the old pagan notion, that to be a man

01:07:57.480 --> 01:08:00.790
is to belong to a race, endowed with reason.

01:08:00.790 --> 01:08:05.640
To belong to it as a specimen, so that the
race or species is higher than the individual.

01:08:05.640 --> 01:08:08.900
This is 100 years before Nazism.

01:08:08.900 --> 01:08:12.810
Which is to say, that there are no more individuals,
but only specimens.

01:08:12.810 --> 01:08:18.190
But, eternity which arches over and high above
the temporal.

01:08:18.190 --> 01:08:20.790
Tranquil as the starry vault at night.

01:08:20.790 --> 01:08:25.330
And God in heaven, who in the bliss of that
sublime tranquility, holds in survey, without

01:08:25.330 --> 01:08:30.080
the least sense of dizziness at such a height,
those countless multitudes of men, and knows

01:08:30.080 --> 01:08:32.400
each single individual by name.

01:08:32.400 --> 01:08:39.850
He, the great examiner says that only one
attains the goal.

01:08:39.850 --> 01:08:43.980
Kierkegaard was a Christian existentialist
Protestant.

01:08:43.980 --> 01:08:46.600
Dostoyevsky was an Orthodox Christian existentialist.

01:08:46.600 --> 01:08:51.970
And Nietzsche, who was also an existentialist,
was a, perhaps the most effective anti-Christian

01:08:51.970 --> 01:08:54.080
philosopher who has ever existed.

01:08:54.080 --> 01:09:00.910
And he made it one of his conscious aims,
to take a hammer to everything that was foundational

01:09:00.910 --> 01:09:04.230
against what was left of Christianity at the
time that he existed.

01:09:04.230 --> 01:09:14.970
The reason I am telling you this is because
existentialism is 

01:09:14.970 --> 01:09:15.970
a strange philosophy.

01:09:15.970 --> 01:09:20.120
It brings people with very divergent fundamental
assumptions together.

01:09:20.120 --> 01:09:22.830
They share certain assumptions.

01:09:22.830 --> 01:09:28.030
And one assumption is that life, in its essence,
is suffering.

01:09:28.030 --> 01:09:34.570
And the second is that the individual has
a responsibility to adopt responsibility in

01:09:34.570 --> 01:09:35.570
the face of that suffering.

01:09:35.570 --> 01:09:41.000
And that is the proper response the proper
response in the nihilism or ideological possession.

01:09:41.000 --> 01:09:46.470
It is something else at something that depends
on the person themselves.

01:09:46.470 --> 01:09:49.180
Nietzsche draws the same conclusions as Kierkegaard.

01:09:49.180 --> 01:09:55.950
A traveller who had seen many countries and
peoples and several continents was asked what

01:09:55.950 --> 01:10:02.920
human traits he had found everywhere, and
he answered, men are inclined to laziness.

01:10:02.920 --> 01:10:05.260
Some will feel that he might have said with
greater justice.

01:10:05.260 --> 01:10:08.710
They are all timid.

01:10:08.710 --> 01:10:12.680
They hide behind customs and opinions.

01:10:12.680 --> 01:10:16.730
At bottom, every human being knows very well
that he is in this world just once, as something

01:10:16.730 --> 01:10:23.100
unique and that no accident, however strange,
will throw together a second time into a unity.

01:10:23.100 --> 01:10:25.460
Such a curious and diffuse plurality.

01:10:25.460 --> 01:10:33.120
He knows it, but hides it like a bad conscience.

01:10:33.120 --> 01:10:36.350
Why?

01:10:36.350 --> 01:10:41.040
The last lecture, when we were talking about
Rogers, not the video lecture, but last time

01:10:41.040 --> 01:10:43.920
I talk to you about Rogers, I was talking
about instrumental speech.

01:10:43.920 --> 01:10:49.920
And that is the speech that you engage in
that is inauthentic from Roger and existentialist

01:10:49.920 --> 01:10:50.920
perspective.

01:10:50.920 --> 01:10:55.810
When the goal of the speech is to extract
something from someone or something.

01:10:55.810 --> 01:10:58.680
The goal is not mere clarity of communication.

01:10:58.680 --> 01:11:04.500
And so what that means is that, the speech
becomes separate from the person.

01:11:04.500 --> 01:11:10.080
And the speech is being used as a tool for
what some element of the person requires.

01:11:10.080 --> 01:11:15.560
And that's the hiding behind convention that
Nietzsche's talking about.

01:11:15.560 --> 01:11:21.270
Because, when people use instrumental speech,
they're almost always pursuing something that

01:11:21.270 --> 01:11:23.990
other people have told them that they should
want.

01:11:23.990 --> 01:11:26.430
It might be status.

01:11:26.430 --> 01:11:28.260
It might be career promotion.

01:11:28.260 --> 01:11:41.380
Those are two of the major, it might be material
progress of other sorts.

01:11:41.380 --> 01:11:47.680
But the problem is that it is only part of
the person talking.

01:11:47.680 --> 01:11:50.630
And that part, is the part that is fixated
on that local achievement.

01:11:50.630 --> 01:11:57.700
It's not the part that is attempting to inquire
about what the truth might be in this particular

01:11:57.700 --> 01:12:00.540
situation, and to describe it as carefully
as possible.

01:12:00.540 --> 01:12:03.910
And that is the clearest speech of the individual.

01:12:03.910 --> 01:12:12.100
Now one of the premises of existential psychotherapy
is that that is the only way you can be healthy.

01:12:12.100 --> 01:12:15.910
You have to learn to speak and act as a whole.

01:12:15.910 --> 01:12:20.280
And you have to be directed towards responsibility
and truth.

01:12:20.280 --> 01:12:22.480
And the consequences of not doing that will
be

01:12:22.480 --> 01:12:30.130
A. that you will suffer pointlessly which
is the worst kind of suffering and

01:12:30.130 --> 01:12:35.190
B. which is worse, you will bring rack and
ruin onto everyone around you.

01:12:35.190 --> 01:12:38.380
And it gets worse than that, actually, because
if you do that long enough.

01:12:38.380 --> 01:12:45.570
Not only will you bring rack in ruin on everyone
around you, you will want to.

01:12:45.570 --> 01:12:50.610
And that seems at least potentially like a
bad outcome.

01:12:50.610 --> 01:12:56.750
Well, you can say, if you adopt a firm belief
system that will protect you from that.

01:12:56.750 --> 01:13:03.370
It is like the terror management idea of ideology
protecting you from death anxiety.

01:13:03.370 --> 01:13:08.690
Which is a, it is a very hopeless philosophy,
that.

01:13:08.690 --> 01:13:12.561
Because it basically suggest that the only
reason people have beliefs is because they

01:13:12.561 --> 01:13:14.200
are terrified without them.

01:13:14.200 --> 01:13:21.740
And that if they engage in any sort of heroic
behaviour, it is merely a fa�ade, which

01:13:21.740 --> 01:13:25.570
in the final analysis is empty, although necessary.

01:13:25.570 --> 01:13:29.390
That is where the idea of positive illusions
came from, essentially, you know?

01:13:29.390 --> 01:13:32.770
That life is so terrible that unless you lie
yourself into tranquility.

01:13:32.770 --> 01:13:37.020
You will be mentally unstable and unhealthy.

01:13:37.020 --> 01:13:43.580
A doctrine for which by the way, there is
no real evidence.

01:13:43.580 --> 01:13:50.660
Anyways, from fear of his neighbour who insists
on convention and veils himself with it.

01:13:50.660 --> 01:13:53.900
But what is it that compels the individual
human being to fear his neighbour.

01:13:53.900 --> 01:13:57.580
To think and act herd fashion, and not to
be glad of himself.

01:13:57.580 --> 01:14:01.790
A sense of shame, perhaps, in a few rare cases.

01:14:01.790 --> 01:14:04.710
In the vast majority it is the desire for
comfort.

01:14:04.710 --> 01:14:09.320
Inertia, in short, that inclination to laziness
of which the traveller spoke.

01:14:09.320 --> 01:14:10.400
He is right.

01:14:10.400 --> 01:14:13.740
Men are even lazier than they are timid.

01:14:13.740 --> 01:14:17.850
What they fear most is the troubles with which
any unconditional honesty and nudity would

01:14:17.850 --> 01:14:21.690
burden them.

01:14:21.690 --> 01:14:26.360
Only artists hate this slovenly life in borrowed
manners and loosely fitting opinions and unveil

01:14:26.360 --> 01:14:29.060
the secret, everyone's bad conscience.

01:14:29.060 --> 01:14:33.910
The principle that every human being is a
unique wonder.

01:14:33.910 --> 01:14:37.660
They dare to show us the human being as he
is down to the last muscle.

01:14:37.660 --> 01:14:39.120
Himself and himself alone.

01:14:39.120 --> 01:14:43.600
Even more, that in this rigourous consistency
of his uniqueness, he is beautiful and worth

01:14:43.600 --> 01:14:48.630
contemplating, as novel and incredible as
every work of nature, and by no means dull.

01:14:48.630 --> 01:14:53.600
When a great thinker despises men, it is their
laziness that he despises.

01:14:53.600 --> 01:15:00.100
For it is on account of this that they have
the appearance of factory products and appear

01:15:00.100 --> 01:15:03.410
indifferent and unworthy of companionship
or instruction.

01:15:03.410 --> 01:15:08.540
The human being, who does not wish to belong
to the mass must merely cease being comfortable

01:15:08.540 --> 01:15:09.930
with himself.

01:15:09.930 --> 01:15:13.920
Let him follow his conscience, which shouts
at him.

01:15:13.920 --> 01:15:17.510
Be yourself.

01:15:17.510 --> 01:15:27.170
What you are presently doing, opining, and
desiring, that is not really you.

01:15:27.170 --> 01:15:39.270
That is s good place to stop, see you Thursday.

